{%- comment -%}
  The colour tokens for one skate-page section, scoped to that section.

  WHY A SCOPED <style> AND NOT AN INLINE style ATTRIBUTE. The tokens have to
  reach descendants that are not the element carrying them — the mission band,
  the level tiles, the product shots — and an inline attribute on the wrapper
  does that fine. What it cannot do is stay readable: five sections each
  carrying four custom properties in a style attribute alongside the spacing
  rules is a line nobody can scan. This emits one rule per section instead, and
  `#shopify-section-{{ section.id }}` is unique because every one of these
  sections declares `"tag": "section"`.

  THE SCRIM IS DERIVED, NOT PICKED. A section with a background image darkens it
  toward the section's OWN background colour, via `color_modify: 'alpha'` — so
  the photo and the flat colour read as one surface. A fixed black wash would
  have meant the colour picker did nothing on any section with an image, which
  is exactly the pair Alex asked for.

  Usage: {% render 'aj-grind-vars', section: section %}

  Accepts: section — the section drop. Reads bg_color, text_color, bg_image,
  bg_overlay and, on the mission section only, band_color.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings
  assign bg = s.bg_color | default: '#0f0f11'
  assign fg = s.text_color | default: '#ffffff'
  assign alpha = s.bg_overlay | default: 55 | divided_by: 100.0
-%}
<style>
  #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .aj-grind {
    --aj-grind-bg: {{ bg }};
    --aj-grind-fg: {{ fg }};
    {%- if s.bg_image != blank %}
    --aj-grind-bg-scrim: {{ bg | color_modify: 'alpha', alpha }};
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if s.band_color != blank %}
    --aj-grind-band: {{ s.band_color }};
    {%- endif %}
  }
</style>
